TI  - Nutritional and Clinical Rehabilitation of Severely Malnourished Children with Moringa oleifera Lam. Leaf Powder in Ouagadougou (Burkina Faso)
AU  - U. Zongo
AU  - S. L. Zoungrana
AU  - A. Savadogo
AU  - A. Traoré
JO  - Food and Nutrition Sciences
PY  - 2013
VL  - 4
SP  - 991
EP  - 997
DO  - 10.4236/fns.2013.49128
AB  - Malnutrition in all its \nforms remains one of the most serious and neglected health problem. This \nlongitudinal study referred pragmatic was carried out, which tested or aimed to \nassess the impact of Moringa leaf powder on the nutritional status of \nmalnourished children. It was a pilot study in Ouagadougou severely \nmalnourished children using Moringa as a nutritional supplement. We \ninvestigated the correlations and the impact of Moringa leaf powder on the \nnutritional status. A sample of 110 children aged 6 59 months were recruited \nand randomly selected and assigned to two treatments Group I and Group II. They \nreceived the CREN’s standard nutritional care diet but the Group I received \nmore dose of 10 g of \nMoringa leaf powder per day. At the end, a significant improvement in the key \nparameters was recorded in both groups. However, the group receiving the Moringa \nsupplement recorded a higher average weight gain (8.9 ± 4.3 g/kg/day, against 5.7 ± 2.72 g/kg/day in Group II) and a quicker \nrecovery rate, with an average stay of 36 ± 16.54 days, against 57 ± 19.20 days \namongst those not receiving the Moringa supplement. There is no significant improvement \nin hemoglobin rate in either group (p = 0.060 Group I, p = 0.063 Group II). Tolerability \nwas considered to be good, as there were no recorded cases of medical \nadmittance, no any occurrence of digestive disorders. The supplementation of \nMoringa leaf powder appears to be effective in improving the nutritional recovery \nof severely malnourished children.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Acceptability and impact on anthropometry of a locally developed Ready-to-use therapeutic food in pre-school children in Vietnam
AU  - T. T. Nga
AU  - M. Nguyen
AU  - Roger Mathisen
AU  - Do TB Hoa
AU  - N. H. Minh
AU  - J. Berger
AU  - F. Wieringa
JO  - Nutrition Journal
PY  - 2013
VL  - 12
SP  - 120
EP  - 120
DO  - 10.1186/1475-2891-12-120
AN  - 23945188
AB  - BackgroundIn South East Asia, concerns exist about the acceptability of peanut-based Ready-to-Use-Therapeutic-Foods (RUTF) for the treatment of severe acute malnutrition (SAM). Therefore, an alternative, culturally acceptable RUTF made from locally available ingredients and complying with local food traditions and preferences was developed. The current study evaluated its acceptability and impact on anthropometry.MethodsThe study was a randomized, two-arm, cross-over intervention trial to test the acceptability of the local product (bar) against a commercially available, peanut-based RUTF paste (Plumpy’nut®). Children (n = 67) from two kindergartens in a rural area of North Vietnam were recruited. The age of the children was between 3 and 5 years.ResultsThe Vietnamese RUTF was well-accepted, although overall acceptability was less than of Plumpy’nut®, with the latter scoring higher on palatability (P < 0.05). In contrast, reluctance to eat Plumpy’nut® was higher than for the Vietnamese RUTF (P < 0.05). Impact on anthropmetrical indices was similar for both RUTF. The nutritional status of the children who consumed the two RUTF over a 4 week period improved significantly, with a mean weight gain of 0.64 (SD 0.27) Kg, and increases in WHZ and HAZ z-scores of 0.48 (SD 0.30) and 0.05 (SD 0.13) respectively (P < 0.01 both). Weight gain was similar between the 2 products (0.32 kg per 2 weeks for both).ConclusionsBoth the commercial Plumpy’nut® and the local produced RUTF were accepted although the harder consistency of the local product might have caused the lower overall acceptance. The promising increase in nutritional status needs to be confirmed in a controlled trial in children with SAM.
ER  - 

