TY  - JOUR
TI  - Localized or centralized control of food production for treating severe acute malnutrition: echoes of a past child survival revolution?
AU  - J. Skordis-Worrall
AU  - Marko Kerac
JO  - Maternal & child nutrition
PY  - 2009
VL  - 5 3
SP  - 195
EP  - 8
DO  - 10.1111/j.1740-8709.2009.00196.x
AN  - 19531046
AB  - Severe acute malnutrition (SAM) affects nearly 20 million children under 5, causing over 1 million deaths per year (Collins et al. 2006; Black et al. 2008) and many more cases of stunting, developmental deficit, and other adverse individual and societal effects (Victora et al. 2008; Prasad et al. 2009).While effective treatments are available, and the need to tackle SAM is well recognized (United Nations 2007), the logistics of addressing a problem of this magnitude are challenging. With the Millennium Development Goals’ deadline fast approaching and the urgency to globalize feeding programmes increasing, this editorial asks whether lessons from history could inform current strategy and decision making. In particular we consider whether the current ‘globalized’ modes of ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) production are optimal in light of existing economic evidence and the lessons learned from the oral rehydration solution (ORS) campaign. The past 20 years have seen significant advances in the way therapeutic feeding programmes manage SAM (WHO 1999; Ashworth et al. 2004). Current international efforts are particularly focused on community-based approaches (Collins et al. 2006; Manary & Sandige 2008), the core principle being to achieve public health impact through high programme coverage. RUTFs are the key innovation making ‘Community Management of Acute Malnutrition’ (CMAM) possible (Briend et al. 1999). These nutrient-dense food pastes are designed to be nutritionally and functionally equivalent to the ‘gold standard’ therapeutic milk (Diop et al. 2003). RUTF can be eaten directly from its container, easily transported, and stored for long periods in tropical climates. The commonest recipe uses a peanut butter base combined with milk powder, sugar, oil and micronutrients (NUTRISET 2006). Bachmann estimates that RUTF comprises 36% of the total cost of CMAM, making it the single biggest contributor to the cost of the intervention (Bachmann 2009). The original vision underlying CMAM was to link emergency-feeding interventions needed to save lives with the developmental approaches needed to prevent children from becoming malnourished in the first place (Collins 2001). Central to this was local RUTF production using local recipes and local ingredients (Sandige et al. 2004; Emergency Nutrition Network and Food and Nutrition Technical Assistance Project 2008). This featured in several early CMAM programmes (Fellows 2003). However, with CMAM scaling up nationally and internationally, the demand for RUTF is rising and the challenges for local production are changing in the face of shifting international policy. While small-scale RUTF production can easily supply local feeding programmes, considerable changes are necessary to provide for a country’s or a region’s needs. Factors such as quality control become increasingly important. Not only must the final product be quality monitored, but also some international manufacturing standards that include provisions such as being able to trace each batch of raw ingredient to the original supplier. Such standards are of course expensive and require a level of capital and logistics that are not always straightforward in the countries where SAM is prevalent. Thus, while delivery is decentralizing over time, there is increasing pressure to centralize and control production. This has important implications. In their book Questioning the Solution: The Politics of Primary Health Care,Werner and Sanders engaged in a similar debate more than 20 years ago (Werner & Sanders 1997). Tackling the problem of diarrhoea rather than SAM, they compared the arguments for home (local) production of ORS vs. the supply of pre-produced (centralized) ORS packets. They describe the main proponents of packet ORS as large DOI: 10.1111/j.1740-8709.2009.00196.x
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - A Large-Scale Distribution of Milk-Based Fortified Spreads: Evidence for a New Approach in Regions with High Burden of Acute Malnutrition
AU  - I. Defourny
AU  - A. Minetti
AU  - G. Harczi
AU  - S. Doyon
AU  - S. Shepherd
AU  - M. Tectonidis
AU  - J. Bradol
AU  - M. Golden
JO  - PLoS ONE
PY  - 2009
VL  - 4
DO  - 10.1371/journal.pone.0005455
AN  - 19421316
AB  - Background There are 146 million underweight children in the developing world, which contribute to up to half of the world's child deaths. In high burden regions for malnutrition, the treatment of individual children is limited by available resources. Here, we evaluate a large-scale distribution of a nutritional supplement on the prevention of wasting. Methods and Findings A new ready-to-use food (RUF) was developed as a diet supplement for children under three. The intervention consisted of six monthly distributions of RUF during the 2007 hunger gap in a district of Maradi region, Niger, for approximately 60,000 children (length: 60–85 cm). At each distribution, all children over 65 cm had their Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) recorded. Admission trends for severe wasting (WFH<70% NCHS) in Maradi, 2002–2005 show an increase every year during the hunger gap. In contrast, in 2007, throughout the period of the distribution, the incidence of severe acute malnutrition (MUAC<110 mm) remained at extremely low levels. Comparison of year-over-year admissions to the therapeutic feeding program shows that the 2007 blanket distribution had essentially the same flattening effect on the seasonal rise in admissions as the 2006 individualized treatment of almost 60,000 children moderately wasted. Conclusions These results demonstrate the potential for distribution of fortified spreads to reduce the incidence of severe wasting in large population of children 6–36 months of age. Although further information is needed on the cost-effectiveness of such distributions, these results highlight the importance of re-evaluating current nutritional strategies and international recommendations for high burden areas of childhood malnutrition.
ER  - 

